Watch the matching path's progress, and panic if it stops

The panel goes silent and stays silent: the last line it shows is a complete
one, so that line was written, and then nothing for the rest of the boot.  A
silent panel cannot say whether the kernel stopped or the console stopped, and
the console is exactly what is suspect: console_write() waits for room in
the console ring buffer (osfmk/console/serial_console.c:555) and the
kernel's own kprintf is the only path that does not go through it.

So the matching rounds are counted, and a thread watches the count.  When it
stands still for fifteen seconds the kernel panics, and the panic screen is
drawn by the console blob straight into the framebuffer: it shows up even
when the console path is what is stuck.  That is the difference between the
two answers -- a panic that fires means the kernel was alive and only the
console was wedged; no panic and no reset means the kernel itself stopped.

Everything lives in this file: the counter, the thread and the bump, so
nothing has to be shared across kernel components.
